    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d814">

                <pb facs="1008" n="884" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg884"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">422.11 G 93/1162: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hartman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-02-18T10:00:00-05:14"><hi rendition="#italic">February 18,
                                1921—10 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received February 19—7:06
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>7. Department’s no. 2, February 14, 7 p.m. Daily deposits resumed with large
                    amounts February 7th. Total amount on deposit February 15th account interest
                    railway bonds 763,500 sucres; salt certificates 145,318 sucres.</p>
                <pb facs="1009" n="885" xml:id="pg885"/>
                <p>The President and Minister of Hacienda are in Guayaquil endeavoring to solve the
                    critical financial problems of the Government and those of the Association of
                    Agriculturalists growing out of the cacao [taxes],<anchor n="16"
                        xml:id="d814-f1"/> They are expected to arrive Quito within a few days. Upon
                    the return of the President I will call upon him with a view to ascertain the
                    results of his efforts in Guayaquil and report to the Department.</p>
                <p>I had an interview with the Minister for Foreign Affairs yesterday afternoon on
                    the subject but he had not learned of the temporary suspension of daily
                    deposits. He stated that it is the policy of President to continue daily
                    deposits in spite of the present critical financial condition of the Government
                    and remit as soon as British exchange can be bought at a reasonable figure.</p>
                <p>I am expecting full information from Mr. Lindberg<anchor n="17" xml:id="d814-f2"
                    /> regarding the negotiations now in progress looking toward the adjustment of
                    the present financial difficulties, and upon its receipt I will report to the
                    Department.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hartman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d814-f1" n="16" xml:id="d814-f1-content">
                <p>For papers regarding efforts to liquidate the debts of the Cacao Growers
                    Association, see <ref target="#pg896">pp. 896</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d814-f2" n="17" xml:id="d814-f2-content">
                <p>Mr. A. F. Lindberg, assistant manager of the Mercantile Bank of the Americas, and
                    representing the Mercantile Bank and the banking firm of Brown Bros.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
